XXX$" 13194139535659 false] [17592186221141 1145 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$. Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2} \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5)$,\n- continuous on $[MATH: [-5,5),]$ and\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5)$, if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$.\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R}$, so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{5}{2}x^{2} - 3) & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{107}{6}) + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{d}{dx}((250(-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2})\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(250(-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2}) \\cdot (-\\frac{nπ}{5}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(-50(-1)^{n})/(nπ)\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)50 (-1) n + 1 n π \\cdot sin ⁡ (n π x 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535659 false] [17592186221141 1145 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous} }$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{107}{6})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535659 true] [17592186221142 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/9b34c017e88b9ed4f80f45223c5093a2f934d249d666b3c1d929718bc05e9882.png)\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x \\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\n- $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\n- $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}.$" 13194139535659 true] [17592186221142 1100 "$[MATH: {-2x, \\mid x ∈ [- 2, 0); x^{2}, \\mid x ∈ [0, 2)]$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $[MATH: x \\in [-2,2).]$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\n1. $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2)$.\n2. $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2)$.\n3. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x)$.\n4. $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$." 13194139535659 false] [17592186221142 1145 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2)$. Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6383/Source/Images/f536c645bf6b1cd0ea1bbc8576eeb69b.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2)$. It consists of two smooth pieces.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4$\nand\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4$.\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x)$.\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6383/Source/Images/b3c8ff64307e627d983401287be632ae.png)" 13194139535659 false] [17592186221142 1145 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/5c1cc57825a318df088ac0bc28ffe75c21579c5fe007fbb52cf0710a1937dc1e.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} 