es term-by-term to get\n> $XXXX$" 13194139535659 false] [17592186221143 1145 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nThus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2} \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4)$.\nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\int_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}t^{2})dt & ∼\\int_{0}^{x}(-4 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(48(-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{2}π^{2})\\cos (\\frac{nπt}{4}))dt \\\\ [- \\frac{1}{4}t^{3}]_{0}^{x} & ∼[- 4t]_{0}^{x} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(48(-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{2}π^{2}) \\cdot [\\frac{4}{nπ}\\sin (\\frac{nπt}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}x^{3} & ∼ - 4x + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)192 (-1) n + 1 n 3 π 3 \\cdot sin ⁡ (n π x 4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535659 false] [17592186221143 1145 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n \\geq 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{[-4t]}_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3} & ∼-4x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{192(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535659 true] [17592186221389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359507 13194139535659 true] [17592186221389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359520 13194139535659 true] [17592186221389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359533 13194139535659 true] [17592186221389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359546 13194139535659 true] [17592186221390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359559 13194139535659 true] [17592186221390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359583 13194139535659 true] [17592186221391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359605 13194139535659 true] [17592186221391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359628 13194139535659 true] [17592186221391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359649 13194139535659 true] [17592186221961 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535659 true] [17592186221961 1100 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [-3,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139535659 true] [17592186221961 1100 "$[MATH: F(x) = {x^{2}, \\mid x ∈ [0, 3); - 3x, \\mid x ∈ [- 3, 0)]$\nThe a