and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-3{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -6x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359561 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359562 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359563 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359564 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359565 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359566 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1041 "field-1" 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359566 13194139535659 true] [17592186359560 1110 #uuid "4f72a895-2a4b-560a-bf08-bc6519036971" 13194139535659 true] [17592186359561 1034 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\